{
  "id": "Compound::DB00722 - Disease::DOID:10763",
  "verbalization": "Lisinopril, an angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE) inhibitor, is implicated in the pathogenesis of hypertension through multiple indirect mechanisms. The evidence suggests that Lisinopril is linked to Dilated Cardiomyopathy, a condition that is itself treated by Spironolactone, which in turn is used to manage hypertension. This creates a chain of treatment relationships that indirectly links Lisinopril to hypertension. Additionally, the evidence indicates that Dilated Cardiomyopathy can be localized to specific anatomical structures, such as the Papillary Muscle of the Heart or the Sinoatrial Node, which are also associated with hypertension. These localized effects suggest a broader systemic involvement of Lisinopril in the development of hypertension, though the exact mechanism remains unclear. The integration of these pathways highlights the role of Lisinopril in influencing cardiac structures that are implicated in hypertension, providing a plausible but indirect link between the drug and the disease.",
